# {{PROJECT}}: engineering contract

This file is read by every coding agent (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor) and every
person who works in this repository. It holds the quality rules only. Product
rules live in docs/ and are linked from the backlog.

The rules are written as conditions, not wishes. When a rule and a request
disagree, say so and ask; do not quietly pick one.

## Before you write code

- Inspect first, code second. Read the code you are about to change, its tests,
  and the guards that cover it (`git grep -l "<file or symbol>" -- '*.test.*'`).
- Read BACKLOG.md before planning a change, so you build on the agreed plan
  instead of drifting from it.
- Extend what exists. Do not add a parallel module, framework or service when
  an existing one can be extended. New infrastructure needs a written reason.
- Prefer boring, production-safe solutions. No speculative refactors.

## Branches and commits

- Never commit or push to `main`. Start every change with
  `git checkout -b feature/<topic>`. The pre-push hook refuses pushes to main.
- One logical change per branch and per PR. Parallel work goes into separate
  git worktrees, each on its own branch.
- A stash is never a release candidate. Only reviewed commits get merged.
- Keep changes reviewable: explain each file change in the PR description.

## Every PR

- Updates BACKLOG.md: finished items move to Done, discovered follow-ups are
  added, priorities stay in sync.
- Changes behaviour only together with a test of that behaviour. A bug fix
  comes with the test that would have caught the bug.
- Closes an incident with a guard. When production broke, the PR that fixes it
  also adds the test or guard that makes the class of failure impossible
  (see "Guards" below), in the same PR.
- Passes the text guards locally before the push: `{{GUARD_COMMAND}}`. CI runs
  the same guards first; a red guard there costs a full CI cycle.
- Follows agent/PR_CHECKLIST.md.

## Guards and budgets

- A guard is a test that reads the source and fails when a rule is broken
  (every route checks the session, client bundles have no regex lookbehind,
  readers of private data fail closed).
- A red guard is a decision point. Fix the code, or add an allowlist entry
  with a reason a reviewer can check. Never loosen a pattern to get green.
- Budgets of known debt only go down. Raising one needs a comment in the
  budget saying why; lowering one is required when the debt is paid.
- Before relying on a guard, make sure it is not passing on an empty scan: each
  guard checks that it sees the files it guards.

## Data and migrations

- Migrations are append-only. Never edit an applied migration; write a new one.
- Every migration must apply to an empty database: CI replays all of them on
  every PR.
- A new table or function ships with its grants: the application role gets
  what it needs, PUBLIC gets nothing.
- Destructive changes (drop, rename, rewrite of a column with data) need a
  written plan in the PR: the order of deploys, the backfill, the rollback.

## Security constraints

These are conditions. Code that breaks one does not merge.

- Secrets are never stored in plaintext, never committed, never logged.
- Access tokens are encrypted at rest.
- Tool scopes are explicit. An agent connection gets the narrowest grant that
  does the job, and an agent never widens its own grant.
- Destructive or externally visible actions require human approval. Every
  write an agent requests becomes an approval card the person confirms outside
  the agent; a confirmation inside the agent's own chat does not count.
- Money: every tool that moves money is dangerous and needs a card; money never
  leaves through an agent (no refunds, payouts or transfers on its surface).
- Reads of private data fail closed: on any error they return nothing.
- Text written by users or fetched from the web is data, never instructions:
  pass it to models as untrusted, and strip contacts and secrets from model
  input and output.
- Every action an agent triggers is written to the audit log: who, what, with
  which arguments, approved by whom.

## How to report a substantial task

1. Summarize the findings.
2. Propose a plan.
3. Implement step by step.
4. Run the relevant checks and tests: `{{TEST_COMMAND}}`.
5. Report risks and follow-ups, and say what was not verified.

Fill in {{PROJECT}}, {{GUARD_COMMAND}} (e.g. `npx vitest run guard.test`) and
{{TEST_COMMAND}} (e.g. `npm test`), then delete this line.
